course: dictionary
lesson: 11638
title: Arm or arms?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The word arms can mean parts of your body, or weapons. The words around it tell you which one the writer means. This is called context.
explain: Look for clues. «She swung her arms as she ran.» Swinging and running are things your body does, so arms means the parts from your shoulders to your hands.
explain: Some phrases have their own meaning. «Dad was up in arms about the muddy boots.» Dad was not holding anything. Up in arms means he was very angry. When you meet a phrase, ask: does this make sense word by word, or is it a special group of words?
fact: An arm is one part of your body, and arms means more than one, or the plural of arm.
fact: «Arms» can also mean weapons, such as guns and swords. This meaning is always in the plural.
fact: The side parts of a chair or sofa are called arms, as in «The sofa has soft arms.»
fact: «Coat of arms» means a special picture or badge that belongs to a family or town.
fact: «Up in arms» means very angry about something. «Arm in arm» means linking arms with someone.
exercise: «She swung her arms as she ran.» What does arms mean here? | A coat of arms | *Parts of the body from the shoulder to the hand | Weapons | The side parts of a chair
exercise: «The soldiers put down their arms and went home.» What does arms mean here? | Badges on a shield | Parts of the body from the shoulder to the hand | *Weapons | Side parts of a chair
exercise: «Dad was up in arms when he saw the muddy boots.» What does up in arms mean? | Ready to go out and play | Happy to help with the cleaning | Holding something high in the air | *Very angry about something
test: «The children ran arm in arm across the field.» What does arm in arm mean? | Fighting with each other | Carrying a ball | *Linking arms together | Running alone
test: «Each town has its own coat of arms on its flag.» What is a coat of arms? | A warm jacket with long sleeves | *A special picture or badge for a family or town | A set of weapons | A chair with arms
exam: «The knight kept his sword and arms ready for battle.» Which meaning fits arms best here? | *Weapons | Shoulders | Parts of a chair | Hugs
